@startuml module-dependency
' EGAS module dependency graph as implemented at the end of Step 5 (commit 1fe679d).
'
' Every edge below is DECLARED, not inferred: each arrow corresponds to an entry in some module's
' @ApplicationModule(allowedDependencies = ...), and ModularityTests fails the build if code creates
' a dependency this graph does not show. The diagram is therefore a rendering of an enforced rule
' rather than an aspiration drawn alongside one.
'
' Two directions are shown because they are genuinely different, and conflating them is the usual
' way a modular monolith's dependency story goes wrong:
'   - solid  = compile-time dependency on another module's published api package
'   - dashed = runtime data flow, which for the event edge points the OTHER way
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam linetype ortho

package "shared kernel" as SHARED_PKG {
  [shared\nIdentifier\nallowedDependencies = {}] as SHARED
}

package "technical" as TECH {
  [platform\nJWT issuance · SecurityConfig · Role\nallowedDependencies = {}] as PLATFORM
}

package "bounded contexts" as CTX {
  [competency\nCompetency Modelling\nallowedDependencies = {}] as COMPETENCY
  [learner\nLearner Profiling\nallowedDependencies = {competency :: api}] as LEARNER
  [gapanalysis\nGap Analysis\nallowedDependencies = {competency :: api, learner :: api}] as GAP
  [catalogue\nLearning Catalogue (stub)\nallowedDependencies = {competency :: api}] as CATALOGUE
  [recommendation\nRecommendation (stub)\nallowedDependencies =\n{competency :: api, gapanalysis :: api, catalogue :: api}] as RECO
}

LEARNER    --> COMPETENCY : competency.api
GAP        --> COMPETENCY : competency.api
GAP        --> LEARNER    : learner.api
CATALOGUE  --> COMPETENCY : competency.api
RECO       --> COMPETENCY : competency.api
RECO       --> GAP        : gapanalysis.api
RECO       --> CATALOGUE  : catalogue.api

COMPETENCY ..> GAP : <<event>>\nCompetencyModelRegistered\n(durable registry, async)
GAP        ..> LEARNER : <<query>>\nLearnerAttainmentQuery\nLearnerIdentityQuery (sync)

note top of SHARED
  Declared to Spring Modulith as a SHARED module
  (@Modulithic(sharedModules = "shared") on EgasApplication),
  so every module may use it without declaring it. It holds
  one type — Identifier — and no behaviour. Keeping it that
  small is what stops a shared kernel becoming the place
  coupling hides.
end note

note bottom of PLATFORM
  allowedDependencies = {} and NOTHING depends on it. Security
  is applied to modules from outside, by one filter chain
  (ADR-015), rather than reached for from inside them. That is
  why no business module can name the Role type, and why both
  web adapters duplicate the ROLE_* authority literals instead
  — an accepted cost recorded in ADR-015 Amendment 1.

  ADR-014: platform carries only the web-adapter ring, no
  domain or application ring, because it has no domain.
end note

note right of COMPETENCY
  Untouched by Step 3 and Step 4 (empty diff under
  competency/src/main across both). Step 5 is the first step
  to change it: +62/-6 across four existing files, plus four
  new ones. Zero deletions, zero renames, and no published
  api signature changed — competency.api gained only new
  types. Measured in the Step 5 completion review.

  ADR-012: EMF lives here and nowhere else. No EObject
  crosses this boundary; the model is compiled to a flat
  snapshot INSIDE this module before it is published.
end note

note bottom of GAP
  The only module with two inbound published contracts, and
  the reason the two edge styles above differ.

  Competency is read ASYNCHRONOUSLY: a registered model is
  projected into gap_analysis by an @ApplicationModuleListener
  travelling Modulith's durable publication registry. The
  compile-time arrow points gapanalysis -> competency; the
  runtime event points the other way, so neither module calls
  the other.

  Learner is read SYNCHRONOUSLY through two published ports.
  The asymmetry is the decision, not an inconsistency in it:
  a competency model is a large, rarely-changing EMF graph,
  while attainment is small, per-learner and wanted fresh
  (ADR-022).
end note

note bottom of RECO
  Declares the edges ADR-006 will need, but gapanalysis.api
  publishes only SkillGapId today, so there is nothing yet to
  consume. Recorded as a stated boundary of the slice rather
  than an implied gap: if Recommendation turns out to consume
  whole reports, GapReportId is promoted from
  gapanalysis.domain.model to gapanalysis.api unchanged.
end note

note as SCHEMAS
  **Persistence isolation (ADR-011): one schema per module,**
  **no cross-schema foreign key, migrations namespaced by range.**

  common          V1–V99     module schemas + Modulith event_publication
  competency      V100–V199  framework_model (jsonb M1 graph, ADR-005)
  learner         V200–V299  profile · proficiency_assertion · evidence_record
  catalogue       V300–V399  (unused)
  gap_analysis    V400–V499  V400 projection (derived) · V401 gap reports (authored)
  recommendation  V500–V599  (unused)

  gap_analysis is the only schema holding both derived and authored
  data, and the split is deliberate: V400's rows are rebuilt by
  replaying an event, V401's cannot be rebuilt at all.

  Cross-context references — competency_id, framework_id, learner_id —
  are identifier values with no foreign key and no existence check
  (ADR-019). Foreign keys INSIDE a schema are mandatory.
end note

note as ENFORCEMENT
  **This graph is enforced, not documented.**

  ModularityTests.moduleTopologyIsValid() verifies the declared
  topology and fails on any dependency not shown here, including
  cycles. Seven ArchUnit rules in HexagonalArchitectureTests enforce
  the ring structure *within* each module — see
  docs/reviews/fitness-function-report.md.

  Nine checks total, unchanged in count since Step 3.
end note

SCHEMAS .. CTX
ENFORCEMENT .. CTX
@enduml
